# Per-video: hook story (concrete scene), pause-and-think question, its answer, 3-line recap.
M={
'c01-big-o':("Your driver works on a test board with ten frames, then the customer streams ten thousand and it falls over. Big O is how you predict that before shipping.","If one loop is O of n and you nest a second loop inside it, what happens to the time when n doubles?","It roughly quadruples: n squared grows four times when n doubles.",["Big O describes growth, not exact seconds","Drop constants; keep the biggest term","Nested loops multiply; sequential loops add"]),
'c02-arrays-pointers':("An interviewer asks: find two prices in a sorted list that add to a gift-card balance. The slow way checks every pair. Two pointers does it in one walk.","In a sorted array, if the two pointers sum to too little, which pointer moves?","The left pointer moves right, because that is the only way to make the sum bigger.",["Arrays give instant access by index","Two pointers squeeze from both ends of sorted data","A sliding window grows and shrinks to keep a rule true"]),
'c03-hash-tables':("You have a million log lines and need to know whether any device ID repeats. Comparing every pair would take all day. A hash table answers in one pass.","What happens to lookups if every key lands in the same bucket?","They slow to O of n, because the bucket becomes one long list to search.",["A hash turns a key into a bucket number","Average lookup is O of 1","Use them for counting, seen-before and grouping"]),
'c04-stacks-queues':("Your editor's undo button and a printer's job line look unrelated. One is a stack, the other is a queue, and interviews use both every day.","Checking balanced brackets: stack or queue?","A stack, because the most recently opened bracket must close first.",["Stack: last in, first out","Queue: first in, first out","Brackets and undo use stacks; BFS and job lines use queues"]),
'c05-linked-lists':("A kernel driver keeps a list of open camera sessions with list head. Removing one from the middle must not shuffle the rest. That is a linked list's superpower.","To find the middle node in one pass, what trick do you use?","Fast and slow pointers: the fast one moves two steps, the slow one moves one; when fast reaches the end, slow is in the middle.",["Nodes point to the next node","Insert and delete are cheap once you are there","Fast and slow pointers find middles and cycles"]),
'c06-recursion-call-stack':("A crash dump shows the same function name two thousand times in a row. That is a recursion with no base case eating the call stack.","What two things must every recursive function have?","A base case that stops, and a step that makes the problem smaller.",["Each call gets its own stack frame","The base case stops the descent","Deep recursion can overflow the stack"]),
'c07-trees-bst':("The Linux device tree, your file system and a company org chart are all trees. Interviewers love them because one idea, recursion, solves most tree problems.","In a binary search tree, which traversal prints keys in sorted order?","In-order: left, node, right.",["A tree has one root and no loops","BST: smaller left, bigger right","Most tree answers are a short recursion"]),
'c08-heaps':("Your scheduler must always run the most urgent task next, even as new tasks arrive every millisecond. Sorting everything each time is too slow. A heap is the answer.","To keep the k largest items from a stream, do you use a min-heap or a max-heap?","A min-heap of size k: the smallest of the top k sits on top, ready to be kicked out.",["A heap always exposes the min or max","Push and pop are O of log n","Top k uses a heap of size k"]),
'c09-graphs-bfs-dfs':("A robot must find the shortest path across a warehouse grid, avoiding shelves. That grid is a graph, and breadth-first search finds the shortest route.","Which search finds the fewest-steps path in an unweighted graph?","Breadth-first search, because it explores level by level.",["Graphs are nodes and edges","BFS uses a queue and finds shortest hops","DFS uses a stack or recursion and explores deep"]),
'c10-dynamic-programming':("Your naive solution recomputes the same answer millions of times and times out. Dynamic programming saves each answer once and reuses it.","What two signs tell you a problem is dynamic programming?","Overlapping sub-problems and an optimal answer built from optimal smaller answers.",["Write down answers to small problems","Define the state and the transition","Top-down memo or bottom-up table"]),
'c11-union-find':("A network has thousands of links coming up one by one, and you must keep answering: are these two servers connected yet? Union find answers almost instantly.","What are the two speed-ups that make union find nearly constant time?","Path compression and union by rank or size.",["Each item points toward its group's leader","Find follows pointers to the leader","Union links two leaders"]),
'c12-tries':("You type three letters into a search box and suggestions appear instantly. Under the hood, a trie walks those letters in three steps.","How long does it take to check a prefix of length m in a trie?","O of m, no matter how many words are stored.",["Each edge is one letter","Shared prefixes share nodes","Great for autocomplete and word search"]),
'c13-bit-tricks':("You read a status register and need to know whether bit five is set, without touching the others. That same masking trick wins interview problems.","What does x AND x minus one do?","It clears the lowest set bit.",["An integer is a row of switches","Masks test, set and clear bits","XOR cancels pairs"]),
'c14-binary-search':("You must find the smallest buffer size that never drops a frame. Testing every size is slow. Binary searching the answer finds it in a few tries.","What property must hold to binary search an answer?","Monotonic: once an answer works, every bigger one works too.",["Halve the range each step","Watch the loop bounds carefully","You can binary search the answer itself"]),
'c15-backtracking':("Sudoku, permutations and seating plans all ask you to try a choice, see if it works, and undo it if not. That is backtracking.","What makes backtracking faster than trying every full combination?","Pruning: you stop exploring a branch as soon as it breaks a rule.",["Choose, explore, un-choose","Prune early","Build the answer in a path"]),
'c16-intervals':("Two meetings in one room clash, and the calendar must flag it. Interval problems are exactly this, and sorting by start time solves most of them.","After sorting by start, when do two intervals overlap?","When the next one starts before the current one ends.",["Sort by start first","Merge while they overlap","A heap tracks rooms in use"]),
'c17-consistent-hashing':("You add one cache server and suddenly every key moves, so the database melts. Consistent hashing makes only a small slice move.","When a server joins the ring, which keys move?","Only the keys between it and the server before it on the ring.",["Servers and keys sit on a ring","A key goes to the next server clockwise","Virtual nodes even out the load"]),
'c18-caching':("A dashboard that took two seconds now loads in twenty milliseconds after one change: a cache. But stale data and eviction rules are where interviews dig in.","Which data structures give O of 1 for an LRU cache?","A hash map plus a doubly linked list.",["Keep hot data close","Eviction picks what to drop","Invalidation keeps data fresh"]),
'c19-queues-backpressure':("Your camera produces frames faster than the encoder can handle, and memory grows until the device dies. Backpressure is how you stop that.","In a single-producer single-consumer ring, who updates the head and who updates the tail?","The producer moves only the head (write index), the consumer moves only the tail (read index), so no lock is needed.",["Queues decouple fast and slow parts","Bound them, or memory explodes","Backpressure slows the producer"]),
'c20-replication':("A server dies at 2 AM, yet no customer data is lost. Replication made that possible, and the interview question is how fresh the copies are.","What is the main risk of asynchronous replication?","Losing the latest writes if the leader dies before followers copy them.",["Copies protect against failure","Sync is safe but slow; async is fast but may lose data","Followers can serve reads"]),
'c21-sharding':("Your user table no longer fits on one machine. Sharding splits it, and choosing the shard key is the interview.","What is a hot shard?","One shard getting far more traffic than the rest, often from a bad key choice.",["Split data by a key","Pick keys that spread load evenly","Resharding is costly; plan ahead"]),
'c22-cap-quorums':("A network cable is cut between two data centres. Does your bank app refuse writes or risk disagreeing balances? That choice is the CAP theorem.","With three replicas, what write and read quorum sizes guarantee you read the latest write?","Two and two, because two plus two is more than three, so they always overlap.",["In a partition, choose consistency or availability","Quorums: reads plus writes greater than replicas","Know your consistency level"]),
'c23-prefix-fenwick':("Your telemetry dashboard needs the total errors between any two minutes, a million times a second, while new errors keep arriving. A Fenwick tree does both fast.","Why is a plain prefix sum array not enough when values change?","An update would force rewriting every later prefix, which is O of n.",["Prefix sums answer range totals instantly","Fenwick trees also allow fast updates","Both are O of log n or better"]),
'c24-monotonic-stack':("For every day of temperatures, find how long until a warmer day. Checking every pair is slow. A monotonic stack does it in one pass.","When a new taller item arrives, what happens to shorter items on the stack?","They are popped, and the new item is their answer.",["Keep the stack sorted","Pop when the rule breaks","Each item pushed and popped once"]),
'c25-topological-sort':("Your build system must compile libraries before the programs that use them. Topological sort gives a valid order, or tells you there is a cycle.","How does Kahn's algorithm detect a cycle?","If it finishes with fewer nodes output than exist, a cycle remains.",["Edges point from prerequisite to dependent","Start with nodes that have no prerequisites","Leftovers mean a cycle"]),
'c26-grid-dp':("A robot can only move right or down across a floor map with obstacles. How many routes reach the far corner? Grid dynamic programming fills the answer cell by cell.","Which cells does each grid cell depend on for right-or-down paths?","The cell above and the cell to the left.",["Each cell uses already-filled neighbours","Fill in order","Often one row of memory is enough"]),
'c27-string-matching':("You search a gigabyte log for one error string. Restarting after every mismatch is slow. KMP remembers what already matched.","What does the KMP failure table store?","For each position, the length of the longest prefix that is also a suffix.",["Never re-read matched text","Build the prefix table first","Linear time overall"]),
'c28-idempotency-retries':("A customer taps Pay, the network drops, the app retries, and the card is charged twice. Idempotency keys prevent exactly that.","Where must the idempotency key be checked?","On the server, atomically, before doing the work.",["Retries are normal","Idempotent means twice equals once","Use keys and backoff with jitter"]),
'c29-cdn-edge':("A live stream starts and five million viewers hit play at once. Without a CDN the origin server collapses.","What is request collapsing at the edge?","Many identical cache misses wait on a single fetch from origin.",["Copies live near users","TTL controls freshness","Shield and collapse requests to protect origin"]),
'c30-transactions-mvcc':("Power fails halfway through a money transfer. After reboot, did the money vanish? Transactions and the write-ahead log say no.","How does MVCC let readers avoid blocking writers?","Readers see an older committed version while writers create a new one.",["All or nothing","Log before you change","Versions let reads and writes overlap"]),
'c31-leader-election':("Two servers both think they are the leader and both write to storage, corrupting it. Leases and fencing tokens stop the old leader.","What does a fencing token protect against?","A paused old leader waking up and writing after a new leader was chosen.",["Exactly one leader at a time","Leases expire","Fencing tokens reject stale leaders"]),
'o20-march-tests':("A board passes every software test but crashes randomly in the field. The culprit is one bad memory cell. March tests are how factories catch it.","Why does a march test go both up and down through memory?","Some coupling faults only appear in one address order.",["Write and read patterns in order","Cover stuck-at and coupling faults","March C minus is the classic"]),
'o20-mcdc-1553':("An aircraft software reviewer asks: prove every condition in this if-statement really matters. That proof is MC/DC.","For a decision with three conditions, roughly how many tests does MC/DC need?","About four, n plus one.",["Each condition must flip the result on its own","1553 is a command-response bus","Bus controller talks, remote terminals answer"]),
'o20-otel-tail':("Your fleet makes a billion traces a day, but you can only store one percent. How do you keep the ones with errors? Tail sampling.","Why can't head sampling keep all error traces?","It decides at the start, before anyone knows the request will fail.",["Head sampling decides early","Tail sampling decides after the trace ends","Keep errors and slow traces"]),
'o20-outbox-saga':("An order is saved, but the confirmation message never reaches the warehouse. The outbox pattern makes that impossible.","Why not write to the database and then publish to the queue separately?","A crash between the two leaves them disagreeing.",["Write data and event in one transaction","A relay publishes the outbox","Sagas undo steps when later ones fail"]),
'o20-sensor-content':("One bad security content update can crash millions of computers at once. This video is about shipping detection updates safely.","What is the safest way to roll out a new content file?","Validate it, then ship to a small ring first and widen only if healthy.",["Track process parent and child","Validate content before loading","Roll out in rings"]),
'o20-vxlan-mtu':("Pings work across the cloud, but big file transfers hang. The cause is a 50 byte VXLAN header and an MTU mismatch.","How many bytes does VXLAN over IPv4 add?","About 50 bytes.",["VXLAN wraps Ethernet inside UDP","Leave MTU room for the header","The forwarding table learns where MACs live"]),
}
